🟢 1. Kubernetes Fundamentals — MUST LEARN
Kubernetes Introduction — Container orchestration and core concepts
Why Kubernetes? — Scalability, availability, automation and self-healing
Kubernetes History — Google Borg → Kubernetes → CNCF
Monolithic vs Microservices — Architecture differences and Kubernetes use
Kubernetes Architecture — Control Plane + Worker Nodes
Control Plane Components — API Server, etcd, Scheduler, Controller Manager
Worker Node Components — Kubelet, Kube-proxy, Container Runtime
kubectl — Kubernetes command-line management
Kubernetes YAML — Manifest structure and declarative configuration
Cluster, Node & Pod — Basic Kubernetes hierarchy
🟢 2. Cluster Setup — MUST LEARN
KIND — Local Kubernetes cluster using Docker
Minikube — Local Kubernetes learning and testing cluster
Kubeadm — Kubernetes cluster installation and initialization
AWS Setup — Cloud environment for Kubernetes hands-on
Amazon EKS — Managed Kubernetes service on AWS

Priority: Learn Minikube/KIND for practice and EKS for real-world DevOps. Kubeadm only needs basic understanding initially.


🟢 3. Kubernetes Workloads — MUST LEARN
Namespaces — Resource isolation and organization
Pods — Smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes
Deployments — Application deployment and replica management
ReplicaSets — Maintaining desired number of Pods
static pod
manual scheduling
Labels & Selectors — Identifying and connecting Kubernetes resources
Rolling Updates — Updating applications without downtime
Rollback — Reverting failed deployments
DaemonSets — Running a Pod on selected/all nodes
Jobs — One-time task execution
CronJobs — Scheduled task execution
StatefulSets — Managing stateful applications

🟢 4. Kubernetes Networking — MUST LEARN
Kubernetes Networking — Pod-to-Pod communication
Services — Exposing Pods and service discovery
ClusterIP — Internal application access
NodePort — Exposing application through node ports
LoadBalancer — External load-balanced access
Service Discovery — Communication between Kubernetes services
Kubernetes DNS — Internal name resolution
Ingress — HTTP/HTTPS traffic routing
Ingress Controller — Implementing and managing Ingress


🟢 5. Kubernetes Storage — MUST LEARN
Kubernetes Storage — Persistent application data
PersistentVolume (PV) — Cluster-level storage resource
PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) — Request for storage
StorageClass — Storage type and dynamic provisioning
Dynamic Provisioning — Automatically creating storage
🟢 6. Configuration & Secrets — MUST LEARN
ConfigMaps — Non-sensitive application configuration
Secrets — Passwords, tokens and sensitive configuration
Environment Variables — Passing configuration to containers
Annotations — Additional metadata and configuration


🟢 7. Resource Management — MUST LEARN
Resource Requests — Minimum CPU/Memory required by a Pod
Resource Limits — Maximum CPU/Memory a Pod can consume
Resource Quotas — Namespace-level resource restrictions
🟢 8. Health Checks — MUST LEARN
Liveness Probe — Checks whether application is alive
Readiness Probe — Checks whether application can receive traffic
Startup Probe — Handles slow-starting applications


🟢 9. Scheduling — MUST LEARN
Taints & Tolerations — Control which Pods run on nodes
Node Affinity — Schedule Pods to specific nodes
Pod Affinity — Place related Pods together
Pod Anti-Affinity — Keep Pods apart for availability
🟢 10. Scaling — MUST LEARN
HPA — Automatically scales Pods horizontally
VPA — Adjusts CPU/Memory resources vertically
Autoscaling Concepts — Scaling applications based on workload

Priority: HPA is very important. VPA can be learned at a basic/intermediate level.

🟢 11. Kubernetes Security — MUST LEARN
RBAC — Role-Based Access Control
Roles & RoleBindings — Namespace-level permissions
ClusterRoles & ClusterRoleBindings — Cluster-level permissions
ServiceAccounts — Identity for applications running in Kubernetes
Secrets Security — Managing sensitive information safely
🟢 12. Kubernetes Troubleshooting — VERY IMPORTANT
Pod Troubleshooting — Finding application and container problems
kubectl get — Checking resources and status
kubectl describe — Investigating resource events/details
kubectl logs — Checking application logs
kubectl exec — Accessing a running container
CrashLoopBackOff — Debugging repeatedly crashing Pods
ImagePullBackOff — Debugging image-pull failures
Pending Pods — Understanding scheduling/resource problems
Service Problems — Debugging connectivity and selectors
DNS Problems — Debugging Kubernetes service resolution
Node Problems — Diagnosing unhealthy worker nodes
CPU/Memory Problems — Identifying resource bottlenecks


🟢 13. Helm — MUST LEARN
Helm — Kubernetes package manager
Helm Charts — Application packaging
values.yaml — Application configuration
Helm Install — Deploying applications
Helm Upgrade — Updating applications
Helm Rollback — Reverting releases
🟢 14. Kubernetes + DevOps — MUST LEARN
Docker + Kubernetes — Containerized application deployment
Git + Kubernetes — Version-controlled manifests
CI/CD + Kubernetes — Automated deployment
GitHub Actions/Jenkins + Kubernetes — Pipeline integration
Container Registry — Push/Pull images for Kubernetes
ECR + EKS — AWS container deployment
Environment-based Deployment — Dev, Test and Production
Rolling Deployment — Gradual application updates
Blue-Green Deployment — Switching between application versions
Canary Deployment — Gradually releasing new versions


🟢 15. AWS EKS — MUST LEARN FOR CLOUD/DEVOPS
EKS Architecture — Managed Kubernetes on AWS
EKS Node Groups — Managing worker nodes
IAM + EKS — AWS permissions and Kubernetes access
VPC + EKS — Networking for Kubernetes
Load Balancer + EKS — External application access
ECR + EKS — Container image deployment
EKS Application Deployment — Deploying a complete application
🟡 16. Advanced Kubernetes — LEARN AFTER BASICS
Init Containers — Initialization before main containers
Sidecar Containers — Supporting containers inside a Pod
CRDs — Creating custom Kubernetes resources
Operators — Automating application management
Kubernetes API — Programmatic cluster interaction
Service Mesh — Managing microservice communication
Istio — Traffic management, security and observability


🟡 17. Monitoring & Logging — IMPORTANT
Kubernetes Monitoring — Cluster and application health
Kubernetes Logging — Application and container logs
Metrics — CPU, Memory and application metrics
Kubernetes Dashboard — Visual cluster management
Observability — Metrics, Logs and Traces

Dashboard is useful, but understanding monitoring + logging + observability is more important.

